        "math_academy_id": "q-130029",
        "problem": "Given $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{4}),$ compute $P(X=3).$",
        "worked_solution": "If $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,\u2026 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(\\frac{3}{4}),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x-1}(\\frac{3}{4}), & x=1,2,3,\u2026 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}(\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
